<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    bg_unread_badge — pill/circle background for unread count
    ===
    Referenced by:
      - item_conversation.xml (tv_unread_badge)
      - future: a "unread in conversation" floating pill in
        ChatActivity (planned for Phase 5; same file reused)

    Shape geometry:
      The consumer view (tv_unread_badge in item_conversation.xml)
      declares width="wrap_content", height="20dp",
      minWidth="20dp", and 6dp of horizontal padding. With those
      bounds:
        - single digit  → width = 20dp = height → <corners> of
                          10dp produces a perfect CIRCLE.
        - two digits    → width = 24dp–28dp → capsule (pill) with
                          10dp rounded ends.
        - three chars   → width = 32dp–36dp → wider capsule.

    Why <corners android:radius="10dp"> and not cornerSize="50%":
      - "50%" corners ARE supported on <shape> in Android, but
        they only produce a perfect circle when width == height.
        For the two-digit pill state we actually want the corner
        radius to remain fixed (10dp = half of 20dp height), so
        the ends stay semicircular regardless of total width.
        A 10dp fixed radius achieves that. "50%" of the *width*
        would over-round wide badges into lens shapes.
      - Do NOT switch to "50%" thinking it will "handle both
        cases better" — it will produce a pill with pointed ends
        (elliptical corners, not circular) for the multi-digit
        case, which looks wrong next to WhatsApp/Messenger badges.

    Why colorPrimary as the default solid:
      - The badge's normal state is the brand accent — a prominent
        visual cue for "you have unread messages." Using a fixed
        @color/pulse_accent would work in light theme but would
        clash with the darker dark-theme surface; using
        ?attr/colorPrimary lets the theme decide (md_primary_light
        in light mode, md_primary_dark in dark mode).
      - ConversationAdapter overrides the tint at runtime ONLY
        for muted conversations, applying colorSurfaceVariant
        instead. For the normal (unmuted) case, NO tint call is
        made — the drawable's default colorPrimary solid renders
        as intended. This is why the tint call is conditional in
        the adapter.
      - Do NOT change the solid to white and rely on setBackgroundTintList
        for both states. White would produce a broken badge in the
        brief window between view inflation and the first tint
        call (visible as a white flash on fast-scrolling
        RecyclerViews on some devices), and the muted-state
        override in the adapter would then require a second tint
        pass for the normal state — extra complexity for no gain.

    Why no <stroke>:
      - A stroke would add visual weight to what is intentionally
        a small, high-density element. The badge sits at the end
        of a 72dp row and needs to read as a compact signal, not
        as a bordered chip. Material 3 badges (used in the
        BottomNavigationView selected-item indicator) also use
        fill-only styling — matching this keeps the whole app
        visually consistent.

    Why height=20dp baked into the view, not this drawable:
      - The drawable does not declare a <size>. It fills its
        view bounds. The view declares height=20dp and the
        corners are 10dp so the ends are perfectly semicircular
        at that height. If a future variant wants a smaller
        badge (say 18dp for a compact list mode), it must
        override both height AND corners in its own drawable —
        do not reuse this drawable with a different height, or
        the corners will be under-rounded (looking rectangular)
        or over-rounded (looking lens-shaped).
    ============================================================
-->
<shape
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">

    <solid android:color="?attr/colorPrimary" />

    <corners android:radius="10dp" />

</shape>
